\begin{note}
Exceptions are just classes,
and the exception hierarchy carries meaning.
For example, \lstinline{RecursionError} is a subclass of \lstinline{RuntimeError},
so an \lstinline{except RuntimeError} block will also catch a \lstinline{RecursionError}.
This is why the recursion quiz problems keep saying \lstinline{except RuntimeError}:
a stack overflow is reported by python as a \lstinline{RecursionError},
which is a kind of \lstinline{RuntimeError}.

The hierarchy of the exceptions you are required to know is:

\begin{lstlisting}
BaseException
`-- Exception
    |-- LookupError
    |   |-- IndexError
    |   `-- KeyError
    |-- RuntimeError
    |   `-- RecursionError
    |-- AttributeError
    |   `-- FrozenInstanceError
    |-- TypeError
    |-- ValueError
    |-- AssertionError
    `-- ZeroDivisionError
\end{lstlisting}

\lstinline{LookupError} is the parent of \lstinline{IndexError} and \lstinline{KeyError},
so an \lstinline{except LookupError} block catches both list out-of-bounds
and dict missing-key errors.
\lstinline{FrozenInstanceError} is a kind of \lstinline{AttributeError},
raised when assigning to a field of a \lstinline{@dataclass(frozen=True)} instance.
\end{note}
